{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-180761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which set is equivalent to $A={k\\in Z:{k}^{2}+1\\le 5}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-180761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of the given set are the integers that satisfy the inequality ${k}^{2}+1\\le 5.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+1 & \\le 5 \\\\ {k}^{2} & \\le 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{4} \\\\ |k| & \\le 2 \\\\ -2\\le k & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integer solutions of the inequality above are $0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the given set can be written as \n\n$$\nA={0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-180761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f67b01e1bef58e177f306ef83bf6e306595405259269b760a1e404d5faa6a737"} {"question" "q-180761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-180761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}"] ["math" "{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,…}"] ["math" "{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2}"] ["math" "{…,-3-2,-1,0,1,2}"] ["math" "∅"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-180761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:{x}^{2}+1=0}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of the given set are the real numbers that satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+1=0.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-1} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x\\in R$ and $\\pm \\text{i}\\notin R,$ we conclude that the set $A$ contains no elements. Hence, $A=∅.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f67b01e1bef58e177f306ef83bf6e306595405259269b760a1e404d5faa6a737"} {"question" "q-181137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "C"] ["math" "{1}"] ["math" "{\\pm 1}"] ["math" "{\\pm \\text{i}}"] ["math" "∅"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "∅"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which set is equivalent to $A={n\\in N:|\\frac{n}{4}|=3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the equation $|\\frac{n}{4}|=3.$ So, we first need to solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{n}{4}| & =3 \\\\ \\frac{n}{4} & =\\pm 3 \\\\ n & =\\pm 12 \\\\ n & =-12,12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $n=-12$ and $n=12.$ However, since $-12\\notin N,$ we conclude that the set $A$ contains only one element, namely $n=12.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given set as\n\n$$\nA={12}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f67b01e1bef58e177f306ef83bf6e306595405259269b760a1e404d5faa6a737"} {"question" "q-181767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[-12.12]"] ["math" "[0,12]"] ["math" "{-12,12}"] ["math" "{1,2,3,…,12}"] ["math" "{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=1}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=1\n$$\n\n are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi x & =2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,…}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f67b01e1bef58e177f306ef83bf6e306595405259269b760a1e404d5faa6a737"} {"question" "q-181787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "Z"] ["math" "{0,2,4,6,8,…}"] ["math" "{0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,…}"] ["math" "{N}_{0}"] ["math" "{\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 4,…}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,…}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which set is equivalent to $A={x\\in R:\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=-1}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=-1\n$$\n\n are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi x & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =1+2n \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n\\in Z.$ \n\nSubstituting different values of $n,$ we see that the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\nA={\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,…}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0187336b919716c1bcce04a50e5c939f3fa0d2bb2a2346d94ad8e330dfebc3f"} {"question" "q-181788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042320/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f67b01e1bef58e177f306ef83bf6e306595405259269b760a1e404d5faa6a737"} {"question" "q-181788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma